# Lesson 3, new example hand: count your winners at no-trump, then set up the extra tricks.
title: Count, then set up clubs
id: L03-01
type: example
lesson: 3
concepts: 3.1, 3.2
student: S
board: 27
dealer: S
vulnerable: none

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  752    A64    K7     KQJT4
  E  63     J983   QJT53  83
  S  AK4    KQ7    A862   762
  W  QJT98  T52    94     A95

auction:
  S 1NT    = 15-17 points, balanced
  W Pass
  N 3NT    = 10+ points: game
  E Pass
  S Pass
  W Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1NT with 16 points, and partner, with 13, raised to game. You
  are declarer, because you bid no-trump first, and so West will lead to the
  first trick.

planning:
  winners: 11
  spades: 2 = The ace and king. Keep one to stop the spades when you lose the lead.
  hearts: 3 = The ace, king and queen.
  diamonds: 2 = The ace and king.
  clubs: 4 = Four, once the ace is gone. Force it out first.
  remind: You need 9 tricks. Count the obvious winners in each suit: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: Where can two more tricks come from?
play:
  1   W SQ  N S2  E S3  S SK
      note: Before playing from dummy, declarer counts. Winners on top: two spades, three hearts, two diamonds. That's 7. Clubs have none yet, because the opponents hold the ace, but once the ace is gone they give four more: 11 in all. The only job is to force out that ace. Declarer wins with the king and keeps the ♠A to stop the spades a second time.
      pause
  2   S C2  W C5  N CK  E C3
      note: Start on clubs right away, while you still hold winners in every other suit.
  3   N CQ  E C8  S C6  W CA
      note: Keep leading clubs until the ace is gone. Losing this trick is part of the plan.
  4   W SJ  N S5  E S6  S SA
      note: West keeps leading spades. That's why you saved the ace.
  5   S C7  W C9  N CJ  E H3
      note: Back to the clubs. They are winners now.
  6   N CT  E D3  S D2  W S8
      note: The clubs have done their job.
  7   N C4  E D5  S D6  W S9
      note: Even the 4 wins: nobody else has a club.
  8   N HA  E H8  S H7  W H2
      note: Now cash your red-suit winners. Hearts or diamonds first: it makes no difference. Here, dummy's ♥A first, then the queen and king in your hand.
      ok: D
  9   N H4  E H9  S HQ  W H5
      note: Over to your ♥Q.
  10  S HK  W HT  N H6  E HJ
      note: The last heart winner.
  11  S D8  W D4  N DK  E DT
      note: The diamonds: your king first, the high card from the short side.
  12  N D7  E DJ  S DA  W D9
      note: Over to dummy's ♦A.
  13  S S4  W ST  N S7  E DQ
      note: West's last spade takes the last trick. 11 tricks: 3NT made with two overtricks.

play recap:
  The plan: count the winners, seven on top and four clubs once the ace is gone,
  and set them up at once by forcing out the ace.

  Notice what declarer did not do: cash the spades, hearts and diamonds
  first. That would have left nothing to stop West's spades when West won
  the ♣A.
